    "issued_at": "2026-09-18T11:17:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "e8a4a6f1-326a-4d66-a44e-a57a28622917",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 181117\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n417 AM MST Fri Sep 18 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil conditions are expected through this weekend \n  with near zero rain chances. \n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures are expected through\n  at least this weekend, with afternoon temperatures near 100\n  degrees across the lower deserts.\n\n- A low pressure system and moisture return early next week will\n  bring a return of rain chances to at least the high terrain of\n  northern and eastern Arizona.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\n\nDrying conditions with near to slightly above normal temperatures\nare expected today through this weekend. It is still muggy at the\nsurface in south-central AZ, including Phoenix, with dew point\ntemperatures in the 60s to near 70 degrees early this morning, but\ndry air has moved in aloft, which is partly why there were no\naddition showers and storms Thursday after the very wet Wednesday.\nPWATs at midnight yesterday were around 1.7-1.9\", but have since \ndropped to 1.0-1.2\" and will continue falling today through \nSaturday. Surface moisture will also steadily scour out today \nthrough Saturday, falling into the 50s by this evening and into \nthe 40s by Saturday evening in south-central AZ. It will be even \ndrier west of Phoenix. The abundant dry air that will be moving\nthrough can be seen upstream on water vapor satellite imagery. \nSatellite also shows a shortwave moving through southeast AZ, \nwhich may provide just enough forcing to spark isolated showers \nand storms in the White Mtns this morning and early this \nafternoon, possibly clipping portions of southeastern Gila County.\nThere is also a broad upper level low situated over the Northwest\nCONUS. This low will gradually track eastward through this \nweekend and help suppress H5 heights over the Desert Southwest and\nthus moderating the temperatures. Temperatures will be nearly the\nsame each day, with morning lows in the 70s and afternoon highs\naround 97-103 degrees across the lower deserts. Normal highs are\nright around 100 degrees for mid-September. These temperatures\nwill be a nice reprieve, especially the mornings, but will still\nresult in Minor HeatRisk. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\n\nHeading into early next week, there is good confidence that a \nPacific low will move to the central/southern coast of California \nby the end of the day Monday. Exactly how this low evolves once\nreaching the coast is still a little uncertain, but latest\nmodel consensus suggests the low will track ENE inland staying \nmostly north of the local area and driving a chance for showers\nand storms across northern AZ. Conceptually this is not a bad\nsetup for a transitional severe weather event, with potential\nfrontogenetical forcing and a moderately strong upper level jet\nwith higher shear, but rebounding quickly from the very dry air \nthis weekend and getting sufficient moisture in here to support \nany lower desert rain chance will be tough. Model guidance does at\nleast support a slow return of moisture Monday through next week,\nmore so coming from the southeast like typical early stages of \nthe monsoon. Model guidance is also pointing toward a strong ridge\ndeveloping through the middle of the U.S. with the subtropical \nhigh restrengthening near/over Texas by mid to late week. This \nwould keep the Desert Southwest under predominant southwest flow \nwhich is not the most ideal setup for lower desert storm chances, \nespecially not for southeast CA and southwest AZ. Latest NBM \nforecast has PoPs focused primarily across northern and eastern AZ\neach day next week with only minor (10-20%) PoPs for south- \ncentral AZ lower deserts in the evenings. The one thing this \npattern does open the door for, the longer it persists, is \npotential Eastern Pacific tropical moisture advecting north.\n\nTemperatures through next week will mostly stay the same day-to-\nday, with highs in the upper 90s to lower 100s, which will be around\n2-5 degrees above normal, and lows remaining mostly in the 70s.\nThe temperatures will lead to widespread Minor HeatRisk through\nat least the first half of the week with a trend toward areas of  \nModerate HeatRisk by the second half of the week as morning low\ntemperatures creep back up.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1115Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period\nunder mostly clear skies. Winds will tend to follow light and\ndiurnal tendencies with a slow turn to westerly winds this \nafternoon and a brief period of southerly winds expected. Wind \nspeeds will generally aob 10 kt.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours under\nmostly clear skies. Outside of extended periods of VRB conditions,\nW/NW winds will be favored at KIPL, while KBLH will favor winds\nout of the S/SW. Wind speeds will be aob 10 kt. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nOverall there are no fire weather concerns through the next week.\nA drying trend will continue today through this weekend. With no  \nrain expected through at least the start of next week. Minimum RH\nvalues this afternoon will be around 20-35% across south-central\nAZ to 10-20% across southeast CA and southwest AZ. The minimum RHs\nwill then dip to 10-20% for nearly all areas this weekend. \nOvernight recoveries across south-central AZ will still be good \ntonight at 45-75%, but then falls lower this weekend. There will\nbe a gradual return of moisture next week, best across eastern AZ\nand there will be an increase in rain chances again, but as of now\nrain chances look to primarily favor northern and eastern AZ high\nterrain. Besides a potential increase in breeziness early next\nweek with a weather system, winds will mostly follow typical \ndiurnal patterns with modest afternoon upslope/upvalley gustiness \n(15-25 mph).\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Benedict \nAVIATION...Berislavich/RW\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict\n",
